compose the menu from a kept picture

The in-game menu ran at 30 fps. On 0046, every menu frame that changed --
the cursor moving, the footer sliding in -- took 24-25 ms of overlay: the
surround and the paused game's rows composed into the canvas, then the
menu's black scrim blended over nearly all of it, then the labels, then
the copy out. The game under it is paused, so all but the labels was a
picture that had not changed.

What an overlay over a still game is composed over is now kept for the
whole canvas, and so is the same picture with the menu's scrim over it.
`fill_rect` blends whole rows and columns, so for every row the scrim
covers, the dimmed copy holds the bytes blending it there would make. A
menu frame is row copies from one or the other, the labels, and the copy
out. The scrim is recognised as the overlay's first sprite when it is a
black rect across the whole canvas width; anything else composes as
before.

Both pictures are built on frames that draw nothing: the core pauses on
the MENU tap and the menu shows 180 ms later, so the still picture is
built on one such frame and, from the scrim of the last visit, its dimmed
copy on the next. The game moving, a repaint or the canvas path drops
them. Two canvases of RAM, 1.7 MB each, while the game stays still.

Measured on the host, release, the menu with its cursor moving every
frame: 0.60 ms a frame before, 0.12 after. On the Flip that would put the
25 ms frame near 5 ms; not yet measured there.

Tests compare against the canvas path pixel for pixel, mirrored and not:
the cursor moving over a still game, and the menu's first frame after
the pause with both pictures already built. With blue light on, the kept
path must match composing the frame from scratch to the pixel, and stay
within the overlay path's long-standing 2 a channel of the canvas path.
Using the dimmed copy for every row instead of the scrim's fails three
of them.

Applies on top of 0047, upstream 91499a9.

